## Test Smell Audit — `CatalogTest.java`

**Framework:** JUnit 5 · **File:** `src/test/java/com/example/CatalogTest.java` · **11 test methods**

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

1 test is clean (`addThrowsOnNegativeCount`).

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH

**1 · Conditional Test Logic** — `addConditionalLogic()` (line 26)

```java
for (String sku : skus) {
    if (sku.equals("b")) {
        catalog.add(sku, 10);
    } else {
        catalog.add(sku, 1);
    }
}
```

The `if/else` branch inside the loop means the test exercises different paths silently. A reader can't immediately see what the expected state is.

**Fix:** Use `@ParameterizedTest` + `@CsvSource`, or just call `add` explicitly for each SKU with no branching:

```java
@Test
void addConditionalLogic() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

**2 · Mystery Guest** — `addLoadsFromFile()` (line 40)

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

The test reads/writes to the real filesystem. CI temp-dir issues, permission errors, or leftover files can cause flaky failures.

**Fix:** Inline the data — no file needed:

```java
@Test
void addParsedInput() {
    String input = "x:7";
    String[] parts = input.split(":");
    catalog.add(parts[0], Integer.parseInt(parts[1]));
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

---

**3 · Sleepy Test** — `addSleepy()` (line 50)

```java
Thread.sleep(200);
```

`Catalog.add` is synchronous and in-memory — no timing dependency exists. The sleep adds 200 ms of waste per run and risks sporadic failures under load.

**Fix:** Delete the `Thread.sleep` entirely:

```java
@Test
void addSleepy() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

**4 · Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)** — `addAndDoNothing()` (line 58)

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
}
```

No assertion at all. This passes even if `add` silently discards the item. It inflates coverage without verifying behavior.

**Fix:** Assert the expected postcondition:

```java
@Test
void addStoresItem() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM

**5 · Eager Test** — `doEverythingAtOnce()` (line 64)

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);  catalog.add("b", 2);  catalog.add("c", 3);
catalog.quantity("a"); catalog.quantity("b");
catalog.inStock("c");  catalog.toString();
assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

Calls 4 distinct production methods (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`). When this fails, you don't know which method is broken.

**Fix:** Split into focused tests — one per behavior:

```java
@Test void addAccumulatesQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1); catalog.add("b", 2); catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
}

@Test void inStockReturnsTrueAfterAdd() {
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("c"));
}
```

---

**6 · Magic Number Test** — `quantityAfterAdds()` (line 77)

```java
catalog.add("a", 41);
catalog.add("a", 1);
assertEquals(42, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

Why 41 + 1? Why 42? The numbers are arbitrary and obscure the intent (testing merge/accumulation).

**Fix:** Use self-documenting values with named constants or obvious quantities:

```java
@Test void addMergesQuantityForSameSku() {
    int firstBatch = 10;
    int secondBatch = 5;
    catalog.add("a", firstBatch);
    catalog.add("a", secondBatch);
    assertEquals(firstBatch + secondBatch, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

**7 · Sensitive Equality** — `toStringFormat()` (line 85)

```java
assertEquals("Catalog(items=1)", catalog.toString());
```

Asserts against the exact `toString()` output. Any formatting change (spacing, field order, field name) breaks this test even though behavior is correct.

**Fix:** Assert on the actual property instead:

```java
@Test void catalogTracksItemCount() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    // toString format is an internal detail — don't couple tests to it
}
```

If `toString` *must* be tested, assert with `contains`:

```java
assertTrue(catalog.toString().contains("items=1"));
```

---

**8 · Exception Handling in Tests** — `addNullSkuManualCatch()` (line 92)

```java
try {
    catalog.add(null, 1);
    fail("should have thrown");
} catch (Exception ex) {
    assertNotNull(ex.getMessage());
}
```

Manual `try/catch/fail` is fragile — catching broad `Exception` can swallow unexpected errors. JUnit 5 provides `assertThrows` for exactly this.

**Fix:** Use `assertThrows`:

```java
@Test void addNullSkuThrows() {
    IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> catalog.add(null, 1));
    assertTrue(ex.getMessage().contains("sku is required"));
}
```

